Every strict approximable map from the Boolean system to an information system equals the conditional of its values at the Boolean elements true and false.

By extensionality of approximable maps, compare relations. If the map relates a set u to a set v, empty entailment of u forces v into the image of the bottom element, which strictness identifies with the bottom element, so v is empty-entailing. Entailment of true or of false produces the corresponding singleton as a witness that each token of v lies in the corresponding value, by monotonicity. Conversely, the Boolean trichotomy applies. In the empty case the relation follows by monotonicity from the empty relation along empty entailment. In the true or false case, v lies in the corresponding value, so some set contained in that Boolean element is related to v, and monotonicity along the singleton token and then along u gives the relation.
